Stop MPI_Type_get_contents converting uninitialised memory

Not an upstream backport, like mpich-abi-f90-datatypes.patch beside it: a local
fix for a defect not reported upstream yet, and worth reporting.

The ABI wrapper for `MPI_Type_get_contents` allocates a temporary for the
datatypes, calls the implementation, and then converts `max_datatypes` of them --
the caller's *maximum*, not the number the datatype actually has:

    MPI_Datatype *array_of_datatypes = NULL;
    if (max_datatypes > 0)
        array_of_datatypes = MPL_malloc(sizeof(MPI_Datatype) * max_datatypes, MPL_MEM_OTHER);
    int ret = internal_Type_get_contents(...);
    for (int i = 0; i < max_datatypes; i++)
        array_of_datatypes_abi[i] = ABI_Datatype_from_mpi(array_of_datatypes[i]);

`MPL_malloc` does not initialise, so every entry past what the implementation
filled is converted from whatever was on the heap. `ABI_Datatype_from_mpi` then
either finds a builtin handle that is not in `abi_datatype_builtins[]` and fires
its `MPIR_Assert(0)`, or decides the garbage is not builtin and hands back a
pointer derived from it. `ret` is not consulted before the loop either, so this
also happens when the implementation failed and filled nothing at all.

MPI-5.0 makes the gap unavoidable rather than a caller error: for a datatype from
`MPI_TYPE_CREATE_F90_{REAL,INTEGER,COMPLEX}`, "an unnamed predefined datatype",
it requires that "an empty array_of_datatypes is returned". A caller that passes
`max_datatypes = 1` and gets `ndtypes == 0` has done nothing wrong, and MPICH's
own suite does exactly that -- `test/mpi/f90/f90types/createf90types.c` calls
`MPI_Type_get_contents(dtype, 2, 0, 1, ints, 0, &outtype)`. On Linux that aborts:

    Assertion failed in file src/binding/abi/mpi_abi_util.h at line 140: 0
    libmpi_abi.so.0(MPI_Type_get_contents+0x333)
    ./createf90types(+0x2cb7)

Reading uninitialised memory is why the same assert has looked intermittent:
`typecntsf`, `typecntsf90` and `typecntsf08` are carried in
ci-scripts/suite/mpich-suite-xfail.txt as `flaky` with this assert as the symptom,
and `createf90types` fails on three of CI's four MPICH Linux variants while
passing on macOS and on `mpich/llvm/linux/24.04/x86_64`.

The fix initialises a pure-out handle array to the null handle before the call, so
the surplus entries convert to `ABI_DATATYPE_NULL` -- which every
`ABI_*_from_mpi` returns immediately, on its first line. Zero-filling would not do:
MPICH's `MPI_DATATYPE_NULL` is `0x0c000000`, and handle kind 0 is
`HANDLE_KIND_INVALID`, which is not `HANDLE_IS_BUILTIN`, so a zeroed entry walks
past the assert into `MPIR_Datatype_get_ptr` and yields a bogus handle quietly.
Only `out` is initialised, not `inout`: an inout array is filled by the wrapper's
own `ABI_*_to_mpi` loop before the call, so all of its entries are already valid.

**This patches the generator, not the generated file.** `install-mpich.sh` applies
its patches and then runs `autogen.sh`, which regenerates
`src/binding/abi/c_binding_abi.c` -- the install log shows
`--> [src/binding/abi/c_binding_abi.c]` -- so a patch against that file would be
overwritten without a word. `maint/local_python/binding_c.py` is the generator
behind it. (`src/binding/abi/mpi_abi_util.h` is not regenerated, which is why the
patch downloaded by URL can target it directly.)

Two wrappers change, `MPI_Type_get_contents` and `MPI_Type_get_contents_c`, and
nothing else: they are the only ones with a pure-out handle array reaching this
branch. `MPI_T_event_get_info` has one too and takes an earlier branch of its own;
the `MPI_Wait*` and `MPI_Test*` request arrays are inout. Verified by regenerating
and diffing the output, which came out as exactly those two hunks.

--- a/maint/local_python/binding_c.py
+++ b/maint/local_python/binding_c.py
@@ -1081,6 +1081,20 @@
                         pre_filters.append("INDENT")
                 pre_filters.append("if (%s > 0) {" % array_size)
                 pre_filters.append("    %s = MPL_malloc(sizeof(MPI_%s) * %s, MPL_MEM_OTHER);" % (name, array_type, array_size))
+                if p['param_direction'] == 'out':
+                    # A pure out array is filled by the impl, which may fill fewer
+                    # entries than the caller's maximum -- MPI_Type_get_contents on
+                    # an unnamed predefined datatype fills none, and the standard
+                    # requires an empty array_of_datatypes there -- and may fill
+                    # none at all if it fails. The conversion loop below runs to
+                    # the maximum regardless, so without this the surplus entries
+                    # are converted from uninitialised malloc: ABI_*_from_mpi then
+                    # either asserts on a builtin handle absent from its table or
+                    # hands back a bogus one. Initialise to the null handle, which
+                    # every ABI_*_from_mpi short-circuits on.
+                    pre_filters.append("    for (int i = 0; i < %s; i++) {" % array_size)
+                    pre_filters.append("        %s[i] = MPI_%s_NULL;" % (name, array_type.upper()))
+                    pre_filters.append("    }")
                 pre_filters.append("}")
                 if p['param_direction'] == 'in' or p['param_direction'] == 'inout':
                     pre_filters.append("for (int i = 0; i < %s; i++) {" % array_size)
